.TH b4-portfolio-list 1  "b4 0.1.0" 
.SH NAME
b4\-portfolio\-list \- List securities positions
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBb4 portfolio list\fR [\fB\-\-account\fR] [\fB\-\-db\fR] [\fB\-o\fR|\fB\-\-output\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-header\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
List securities positions
.PP
List the positions of the securities accounts, ordered by account and name: account, ISIN, name, quantity, the latest price with its currency and day, the purchase price with its currency and day, and the state.
.PP
Only what the vault stores is shown. b4 computes no market value and no gain: a price can be quoted per unit or in percent of a face amount and in another currency than the account\*(Aqs, and the vault does not hold what it takes to turn that into an amount. `b4 sql` on the tables portfolio_items and stock_prices is the place for such sums.
.PP
With \-\-output json, jsonl or csv every position has these fields, named as in `b4 schema portfolio_items`: account_id, id, account_name, isin, name, quantity, quantity_type, quantity_currency, price_type, current_price, current_price_currency, current_price_date, purchase_price, purchase_price_currency, purchase_price_date, state, deleted. Quantities and prices are exact there; the table shows the quantity with as many decimals as the listed positions need.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-account\fR \fI<ACCOUNT>\fR
Only positions of this account

ACCOUNT is tried in this order: the numeric id from `b4 accounts list`; the IBAN (blanks and case do not matter); the account number; a part of the account name (case does not matter) that only one account has, where a complete name wins over a part. If several accounts match, the command fails and lists them with their ids.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-db\fR \fI<PATH>\fR [default: ./b4.duckdb]
Database file: written by `decrypt`, read by every other command
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBB4_DB\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fI<FORMAT>\fR
Output format [default: table]

table is made for reading: line breaks, control characters and text direction overrides in the data are replaced. csv, jsonl and json are data: written to a file or a pipe they are exact, byte for byte. Only when they are written to a terminal are the characters a terminal acts on (escape sequences, text direction overrides) made harmless: csv shows U+FFFD in their place, json and jsonl write them as \\uXXXX escapes.

In json and jsonl a number that a double cannot hold exactly is a string of its digits: an integer beyond 2^53, a decimal of more than 15 significant digits.
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
table: Aligned text, fitted to the terminal
.IP \(bu 2
json: One JSON array of objects
.IP \(bu 2
jsonl: One JSON object per line
.IP \(bu 2
csv: Comma\-separated values with a header line
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-header\fR
Leave out the header line of table and csv output
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Do not print informational messages to stderr

Silences progress and summary lines and the "no rows" note. Warnings and errors are still printed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
b4 portfolio list
    List the positions of all securities accounts.
b4 portfolio list \-\-account depot \-\-output json
    The positions of one account, as JSON.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBb4\fR(1),
\fBb4\-portfolio\fR(1)
